WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:06.800
Hi, I'm Nat.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.500
I was one of the original designers of the Xbox.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:09.984
I've been working with

00:00:09.984 --> 00:00:14.684
with computers since I was maybe seven years old and now I work on virtual reality.

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:22.548
(upbeat music)

00:00:25.768 --> 00:00:27.768
- Computers are everywhere.

00:00:27.768 --> 00:00:29.648
They're in people's pockets.

00:00:29.953 --> 00:00:35.953
people's cars, will have them on their wrists, they might be in your backpack right now.

00:00:35.953 --> 00:00:38.953
But, what makes a computer a computer?

00:00:38.953 --> 00:00:39.937
What does make a computer a computer?

00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:40.937
computer anyway.

00:00:40.937 --> 00:00:47.257
And how does it even work?

00:00:47.257 --> 00:00:49.897
As humans, we've always built tools to help us solve problems.

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:54.922
tools like a wheelbarrow, a hammer, or a printing press, or a tractor trailer.

00:00:54.922 --> 00:00:58.922
All of these inventions helped us with manual work.

00:00:58.922 --> 00:00:59.907
Over time, people are

00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:04.347
People began to wonder if a machine could be designed and built to help us with the

00:01:04.347 --> 00:01:09.891
thinking work we do, like solving equations or tracking the stars in the sky.

00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:14.711
Rather than moving or manipulating physical things like dirt and stone, these machines

00:01:14.711 --> 00:01:19.791
would need to be designed to manipulate information.

00:01:19.876 --> 00:01:25.876
The earliest computers were made out of wood and metal with mechanical levers and gears.

00:01:25.876 --> 00:01:29.860
By the 20th century, though, computers started using electrical.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:35.180
components. These early computers were really large and really slow. A computer

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:39.845
the size of a room might take hours just to do a basic math problem.

00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:46.845
These machines are things of gleaming, very colored metal and numerous flashing lights.

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:54.789
already really awesome at the time and they were only manipulating numbers back then.

00:01:54.789 --> 00:01:59.789
But now we can use them to talk to each other, we can use them to play games, control robots.

00:01:59.814 --> 00:02:04.314
robots and do any crazy thing that you could probably imagine.

00:02:04.314 --> 00:02:08.834
The computers we use today look really different from the earliest thinking machines.

00:02:08.834 --> 00:02:09.794
And who knows what the computer

00:02:09.799 --> 00:02:14.359
of tomorrow will be like. My hope is that you get to help decide what you want the computers

00:02:14.359 --> 00:02:15.399
of tomorrow to look like.

00:02:19.783 --> 00:02:21.863
(chimes)
